160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (In recent years, the consumption landscape in Portugal has experienced a profound shift with the rise of online gambling, a phenomenon increasingly driven by the rapid digitalization of everyday entertainment and mobile financial systems. According to foundational research in consumer behavior, online platforms have detached traditional games of chance from physical venues, seamlessly embedding wagering into the daily routines and "dead time" of modern users. This digital transition is built upon a structural framework of 24/7 availability, operational speed, and unprecedented privacy \(Gainsbury, 2015\). From a consumer behavior standpoint, these specific attributes work together to lower both the friction and the cognitive barriers to entry. By fundamentally altering how young individuals perceive risk, anonymity, and personal control, this frictionless ecosystem has created an incredibly fertile ground for the widespread normalization of gambling practices across the country \(Gainsbury, 2015; Hubert & Griffiths, 2018\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Young adults aged 18 to 34 are at the absolute center of this ecosystem, largely due to their constant exposure to algorithmic social media feeds, aggressive digital advertising, and mobile fintech solutions \(Farias & Antunes, 2025\). In Portugal, an intense sports culture heavily anchored in football has allowed sports betting to weave itself into youth socialization, turning a significant financial risk into a celebrated social ritual \(Calado et al., 2020\). Despite the visibility of this trend, we still lack a granular understanding of how deeply embedded this habit really is in their mindset.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Consequently, this study is guided by a straightforward question: How normalized is online gambling among young adults in Portugal? To explore this, our project uses semi-structured interviews with 47 participants to analyze the lived experiences, individual rationalizations, and social perceptions of both active bettors and non-bettors. By gathering these firsthand qualitative insights, the primary goal of this research is to investigate the mechanisms that drive or restrict this normalization within youth social networks. Finally, to address the current gap in preventive infrastructure, this study evaluates how young Portuguese gamblers react to the prospective integration of Smart Health technologies, specifically investigating whether they would welcome or reject wearable devices designed to provide real-time biometric biofeedback, such as heart rate and stress alerts, during active betting sessions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
