167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 131 0 R
/T (THE SHIFT FROM PHYSICAL TO DIGITAL GAMBLING)
/E (THE SHIFT FROM PHYSICAL TO DIGITAL GAMBLING)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Games and gambling have been long-standing societal practices, with historical evidence tracing wagering as far back as the Hittites \(4000 BC\) and later as a state revenue source during the reigns of Queen Elizabeth and Queen Anne \(McMillen, 1996\). However, the gambling landscape has shifted radically from physical venues to the palm of the hand. As Gainsbury \(2015\) argues, this is not merely a change of scenery but a fundamental disruption of how people gamble. By removing the need for physical presence, online platforms have integrated betting into the "dead time" of daily life, what used to be a deliberate trip to a casino is now a private, immediate, and constant possibility.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (In Portugal, this transition found fertile ground in a country with a long-standing relationship with offline games like the Lotaria Nacional and Placard. The digital market expanded rapidly following regulation, a trend accelerated by the COVID-19 pandemic. During confinement, while traditional casinos closed, Portugal saw over 435,000 active online players and a 47.2% spike in self-restriction requests \(Machado et al., 2021\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
