172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 132 0 R
/T (THE PSYCHOLOGY OF FRICTIONLESS TRANSACTIONS)
/E (THE PSYCHOLOGY OF FRICTIONLESS TRANSACTIONS)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (From a consumer behavior standpoint, the danger lies in how "frictionless" the digital gambling experience has become. The digitalization of money, especially through seamless local tools like MBWay, Revolut, and Apple Pay, creates a psychological disconnect between spending and consequence. When users are not handling physical cash, the "pain of paying" diminishes significantly: money feels less real, lowering the threshold for impulsive bets \(Gainsbury, 2015\). This reduced friction, combined with 24/7 accessibility and the absence of social observation, creates an environment where impulsive spending can escalate without immediate psychological resistance.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 132 0 R
/T (NEUROCOGNITIVE VULNERABILITY TO GAMBLING)
/E (NEUROCOGNITIVE VULNERABILITY TO GAMBLING)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (This vulnerability is compounded by high comorbidity rates: a systematic meta-analysis by Lorains, Cowlishaw, and Thomas \(2011\) found that over 60% of individuals with gambling problems suffer from concomitant psychiatric conditions, including nicotine dependence \(60.1%\), substance use disorder \(57.7%\), mood disorders \(37.9%\), and anxiety disorders \(37.4%\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 132 0 R
/T (FOOTBALL CULTURE AS A NORMALIZATION VEHICLE)
/E (FOOTBALL CULTURE AS A NORMALIZATION VEHICLE)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (In Portugal, an intense sports culture heavily anchored in football has allowed sports betting to weave itself into youth socialization. Unlike casino gambling, which carries social stigma, football betting benefits from association with a mainstream, celebrated activity. Club sponsorships, player endorsements, and in-match advertising have embedded betting brands into the fabric of the sport itself, transforming what is fundamentally a financial risk into a socially acceptable, even expected, ritual among young adults \(Calado et al., 2020\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (This process is consistent with Bandura's \(1986\) Social Cognitive Theory, which proposes that individuals acquire behavioral patterns through observational learning. When gambling is repeatedly modeled by trusted figures such as athletes, celebrities, and peers within a celebrated cultural context, young adults internalize positive outcome expectancies and normalize the behavior before ever consciously evaluating its risks. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 132 0 R
/T (THE REGULATORY GAP: SMART HEALTH INTERVENTIONS)
/E (THE REGULATORY GAP: SMART HEALTH INTERVENTIONS)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (Portuguese gambling legislation \(Decree-Law 66/2015\) established the legal framework for online gaming, focusing primarily on taxation, licensing, and technical compliance. While the regulation created a structured market, it offers limited harm-reduction mechanisms beyond voluntary self-exclusion. Notably absent is any consideration of "Smart Health" interventions, real-time biometric feedback systems that could alert users to physiological stress during active betting sessions.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (Most young adults now wear devices capable of monitoring heart rate and stress levels. Yet it remains unclear whether gamblers notice these physiological signals during betting, or whether they would accept technological "nudges" to pause. This gap, between available wearable technology and its application in harm reduction, represents both a research opportunity and a policy blind spot that this study aims to address.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (Methodology)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
