201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (This study presents inherent limitations. The convenience sampling strategy may introduce selection bias, as participants from the authors' networks may share similar backgrounds. The sample also skews toward higher education \(Nova IMS students\), potentially limiting generalizability to the broader Portuguese youth population. Additionally, self-reported gambling behavior may be subject to social desirability bias regarding frequency and losses.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (Ethically, guidelines were strictly prioritized. To protect participant privacy, all direct personal identifiers were removed, and participants were anonymized using ID numbers. In alignment with institutional data policies, all recordings are securely stored and will be permanently deleted after a mandatory retention period of three months.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 133 0 R
/T (Findings)
/E (Findings)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (The data reveals that the majority of participants \(91%\) perceive that online gambling is either a fully normalized habit of daily life or that it is an activity becoming increasingly normalized in Portugal. Instead of being an isolated or hidden activity, online betting has become a normalized part of young adults' social routines. To understand the specific factors driving this widespread trend, the interview responses were analyzed based on key themes. The following sections present these findings, breaking down the environmental, social, technological, and psychological elements that have combined to lower the traditional barriers and perceived risks of gambling. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (7)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 134 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [528 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 134 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [529 0 R 0]
/K [1]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (The primary channels of exposure identified were interpersonal and broadcast-based, with 89% of participants mentioning both friends and broadcast media, followed by targeted advertising \(40%\) and social media platforms \(39%\). This multi-platform saturation ensures that exposure is continuous rather than episodic. As Participant 11 noted, "I see betting ads on TV, during football streams, on Instagram, Facebook, and YouTube videos." The sense of inescapability was frequently emphasised: "From football to prime-time news, the exposure is constant. We are being completely bombarded with betting advertisements across all entertainment media" \(Participant 4\).)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (2.  FOOTBALL AS A KEY DRIVER OF BETTING NORMALIZATION)
/E (2.  FOOTBALL AS A KEY DRIVER OF BETTING NORMALIZATION)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (The intersection of football culture and betting emerged as one of the most significant findings, with 44 out of 47 participants highlighting the symbiotic relationship between football fandom and gambling platforms. Considering the cultural significance and social reach of football in Portugal, the persistent presence of betting brands within football contexts may intensify the normalization of online gambling and expand its social impact among young adults. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
