>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (3.  SOCIAL INFLUENCE)
/E (3.  SOCIAL INFLUENCE)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Peer influence emerged as a near-universal factor, with all participants acknowledging that friends and social groups significantly impact betting decisions. The research identified peer pressure as both a direct mechanism, friends actively encouraging bets, particularly around football matches, and an indirect one, through the fear of missing out. As Participant 41 observed: "I think it's very influential, especially because of the fear of missing out, which is something huge in our generation.")
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (Social media influencers compound this dynamic by actively constructing an image of easy success and financial accessibility that resonates strongly with younger demographics. Participant 39 noted that "they promise easy money, which creates a sense of opportunity for younger people." A recurring and structurally important theme is the asymmetric communication of outcomes: participants consistently reported that social circles and online content amplify wins while losses remain invisible, producing a distorted perception of profitability and control. "Victories are much more prominently displayed than defeats, and this encourages people to say, 'If they can do it, we can too'" \(Participant 6\).)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (The cumulative effect of these dynamics creates a powerful feedback loop. Once betting becomes embedded in a social group's rituals, continued participation requires less conscious decision-making, the behaviour sustains itself through social reinforcement. As Participant 40 described: "If you're in this group and your friends talk about betting, you'll get that urge to want to get into it too.")
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (4.  DIGITAL ACCESS REDUCES BARRIERS AND ADDS IMPULSIVITY)
/E (4.  DIGITAL ACCESS REDUCES BARRIERS AND ADDS IMPULSIVITY)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (All 47 participants noted that digital payment methods fundamentally alter the psychological experience of spending money on gambling. The seamless integration of tools such as MBWay, Revolut, and Apple Pay removes the "pain of paying", money becomes an abstract number rather than a tangible resource, lowering the threshold for impulsive deposits. As Participant 4 described: "It's the ease with which people open their phones, open the app and in three seconds make a transfer via MB Way, Revolut, QR Code or Apple Pay.")
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
