/P 135 0 R
/T (5. CULTURE MASKS ADDICTION)
/E (5. CULTURE MASKS ADDICTION)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (The interviews reveal that 51% of participants perceive gambling as fully normalised in Portugal, while 40% view it as a rapidly growing phenomenon, and only 9% consider it non-problematic. This widespread acceptance is not merely a product of digital convenience but is reinforced by deep historical continuity and strategic commercial framing.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Participants frequently invoked Portugal's long tradition of state-sanctioned gambling, from Totobola to the Lotaria Nacional, as the cultural foundation upon which digital betting has expanded. As Participant 4 stated: "Betting has always been normal in Portugal. In the past it wasn't on platforms, but on Totobola and things like that. Now, with platforms, this has become easier." This historical familiarity lowers psychological resistance to online forms of the same behaviour.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (This cultural normalisation is actively sustained by high-profile celebrity endorsements and institutional sponsorships that confer legitimacy upon gambling platforms. "Major public figures, like Cristina Ferreira, openly advertise it, which completely normalises the activity" \(Participant 24\). The use of harm-reduction slogans further obscures underlying risks: as Participant 29 observed, "We have a heavy betting culture disguised by phrases like 'gamble in moderation.' Young adults increasingly view gambling not as a risk, but as a legitimate strategy for extra income.")
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Regarding digital guardrails, participant feedback was mixed: while some saw value in biometric or spending-limit interventions, 34% remained sceptical. As Participant 30 noted, "I don't think it's this app or this pop-up that's going to make you want to stop. Stopping any addiction comes much more from the person themselves than from applications.")
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 135 0 R
/T (INSIGHTS)
/E (INSIGHTS)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 135 0 R
/T (1. PARTICIPANTS EXPRESSED THAT GAMBLING NORMALIZATION BEGINS LONG BEFORE ANY ACTIVE DECISION TO BET)
/E (1. PARTICIPANTS EXPRESSED THAT GAMBLING NORMALIZATION BEGINS LONG BEFORE ANY ACTIVE DECISION TO BET)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Extrapolating the empirical findings to young adults in Portugal demonstrates that for the vast majority, the normalization of online gambling is a cumulative, long-term process that occurs long before an individual makes an active, conscious decision to place a bet. Participants specifically highlighted experiencing exposure as young children while witnessing the gambling habits of their loved ones. This early exposure, combined with continuous contact with a high volume of pervasive media through football sponsorships, prime-time television, and social media platforms, transforms gambling from an unfamiliar financial risk into a routine, recognizable element of daily life. As several participants observed, this exposure occurs so repeatedly and from such an early age that gambling feels familiar and routine before any conscious choice is made.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (Furthermore, evaluating the psychological mechanics of a habit loop, it is evident that routines require a recurring trigger to sustain themselves. The contemporary digital and media ecosystem in Portugal acts as an ambient, unavoidable trigger network. As Participant 38 reflected, repeated exposure eventually dissolves the capacity to critically evaluate the behavior. This confirms that normalization is not the result of a single decision point, but of a cumulative, largely unconscious process embedded in everyday media consumption. Consequently, normalization precedes active participation because the environment provides a continuous loop of triggers that naturally integrates gambling into the consumer's subconscious social routines.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 136 0 R
/T (2. PARTICIPANTS IDENTIFIED FOOTBALL AS A CULTURAL MECHANISM THAT SEPARATES BETTING FROM ITS PERCEIVED RISK)
/E (2. PARTICIPANTS IDENTIFIED FOOTBALL AS A CULTURAL MECHANISM THAT SEPARATES BETTING FROM ITS PERCEIVED RISK)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (The empirical findings demonstrate that football acts as a powerful cultural mechanism that cognitively dissociates online betting from its inherent financial risks. Participants consistently distinguished sports wagering from traditional casino gambling. This distinction is rooted in the emotional familiarity and societal celebration surrounding football culture. As Participant 33 noted, peer groups routinely frame football betting as "just for fun," while isolating casino games as predatory environments.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (Additionally, the data captures a distinct cognitive bias within youth consumer behavior. Participant 6 conceptualized this as a "fictitious control," where consumers mistakenly believe that their subjective sports knowledge or team statistics grant them a genuine advantage over traditional gambling in casinos. Because young adults believe they can predict match outcomes based on expertise, they perceive the behavior as highly controllable, which dramatically lowers the perceived risk of placing a bet.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 136 0 R
/T (3. PARTICIPANTS REPORTED THAT SOCIAL BELONGING, NOT FINANCIAL MOTIVATION, IS THE PRIMARY ENTRY POINT INTO GAMBLING)
/E (3. PARTICIPANTS REPORTED THAT SOCIAL BELONGING, NOT FINANCIAL MOTIVATION, IS THE PRIMARY ENTRY POINT INTO GAMBLING)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (Analyzing the interview data unraveled that social belonging, rather than immediate financial motivation, serves as the primary entry point into online gambling for young adults. Multiple participants described initiating betting behaviors not out of an independent desire for money, but to participate in peer conversations, maintain social alignment, and avoid feeling excluded. As Participant 39 noted, shared living and social environments create an atmosphere where peers naturally "end up betting together in everyday life," turning a financial activity into a collective social ritual.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (This dynamic is heavily accelerated by the Fear of Missing Out \(FOMO\), a powerful psychological trigger in youth consumer behavior. When betting becomes a staple of a peer group's routines, non-participating individuals experience a distinct social anxiety regarding both missing out on shared experiences and the potential rewards celebrated by the group. Participant 41 highlighted the speed of this social assimilation, noting it would "probably only take two or three times hanging out" with betting friends before an individual succumbs to the pressure and joins the activity to maintain group cohesion.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 136 0 R
/T ( 4. PARTICIPANTS CALLED FOR STRICTER ADVERTISING REGULATION AS THE MOST URGENT STRUCTURAL CHANGE)
/E ( 4. PARTICIPANTS CALLED FOR STRICTER ADVERTISING REGULATION AS THE MOST URGENT STRUCTURAL CHANGE)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (A compelling pattern that emerged in the analysis is the spontaneous demand of participants for stricter regulatory control over the gambling industry. Without interviewers prompting participants, these young adults consistently shifted the conversation to criticize the current marketing landscape as aggressive and predatory. This consensus highlights a deep consumer preoccupation, where individuals actively recognize that the digital advertising ecosystem exploits young adults and teenagers. They note that promotional campaigns intentionally leverage psychological vulnerabilities through constant exposure, peer pressure, and FOMO.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
