<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 137 0 R
/T (5. PARTICIPANTS EXPRESSED DEEP SCEPTICISM ABOUT TECHNOLOGICAL SOLUTIONS AS STANDALONE RESPONSES TO ADDICTION)
/E (5. PARTICIPANTS EXPRESSED DEEP SCEPTICISM ABOUT TECHNOLOGICAL SOLUTIONS AS STANDALONE RESPONSES TO ADDICTION)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (Besides criticizing the legislation restrictions on gambling exposure, the responses also revealed a deep skepticism toward standalone technological features as effective solutions for gambling addiction. When evaluating potential wearable interventions, such as smartwatch notifications or biometric stress alerts, the vast majority of participants, including active bettors, reported that they would simply ignore or dismiss the prompts. This dismissive behavior highlights how repetitive automated warnings lose their psychological impact and are filtered out by the consumer as mere background noise. As Participant 30 noted, despite wearing a smartwatch that actively prompts him to stop certain behaviors, he could "count on his fingers" the rare moments he actually complied.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (This resistance can be explained when looking at how our brains process decisions, through the Dual-Process Theory, which points out that human decision-making is governed by two cognitive systems: a fast, subconscious, and impulsive "System 1" and a slow, deliberate, and rational "System 2." In the context of digital gambling, the frictionless design of betting apps keeps the consumer heavily locked into an automated System 1 state. Participant 21 accurately stated that overcoming gambling requires real psychological support rather than a digital device. A brief smartwatch alert is just too weak to break that impulsive autopilot loop and force a user to switch over to their rational System 2 thinking. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
