/P 137 0 R
/E (The findings of this study carry implications across three levels: practical, policy, and theoretical.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 137 0 R
/T (PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS)
/E (PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (Given that normalization appears to begin long before adulthood, harm-reduction efforts may benefit from being directed upstream. Embedding financial literacy programs and awareness initiatives within secondary education could help reach adolescents before behavioral conditioning is fully established. Since participants indicated that social belonging, rather than financial motivation, tends to drive entry into gambling, interventions that address peer dynamics may prove more effective than those centered solely on individual risk awareness. Additionally, as participants expressed greater receptiveness to financial transparency tools than to biometric alerts, platform designers might consider prioritizing cumulative loss summaries, similar to those already found in banking applications, as a way of reintroducing psychological friction into the betting experience.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 137 0 R
/T (POLICY IMPLICATIONS)
/E (POLICY IMPLICATIONS)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (Participants spontaneously compared Portugal's advertising landscape to more restrictive frameworks in countries such as Spain, Italy, and the United Kingdom, suggesting that extending regulations to digital platforms, revisiting celebrity endorsement practices, and considering watershed rules for gambling advertisements could be worthwhile areas for policy discussion. The football-betting relationship also emerged as a particularly significant normalization pathway in participants' accounts, indicating that the role of betting sponsorships within professional football may deserve closer regulatory consideration.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
