265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (This study set out to investigate how cultural normalization drives the high popularity of online gambling among young adults in Portugal. The findings consistently point to a phenomenon that is structural rather than individual, normalization is not the result of personal weakness or isolated choices, but of an ecosystem in which advertising, football culture, social dynamics, and frictionless digital access work together to make betting feel ordinary before it is ever consciously evaluated.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Exposure begins in childhood and accelerates through adolescence, meaning that by the time young adults encounter online betting platforms, the behaviour already carries cultural familiarity. Football amplifies this process by lending gambling its social legitimacy, while peer groups sustain it through visibility of wins and invisibility of losses. Digital payment systems remove the final psychological barriers, transforming betting from a deliberate act into a seamless habit embedded in daily life.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Taken together, these mechanisms reveal a significant gap between how gambling is perceived, as entertainment, social participation, or a skill-based activity, and what it structurally is: a financially asymmetric system in which the house always wins. Addressing this gap requires moving beyond individual responsibility. As participants themselves made clear, the most urgent interventions are structural: stricter regulation of advertising, greater transparency around financial losses, and a cultural reckoning with the normalisation that institutional endorsement and celebrity promotion have made possible.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
