272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (This study offers qualitative support for Bandura's \(1986\) Social Cognitive Theory in the gambling normalization context, suggesting that observational learning may function as a pre-decisional conditioning mechanism. The findings also appear consistent with the Dual-Process Theory framework, indicating that frictionless platform design may systematically reduce the engagement of rational System 2 thinking. Finally, the skepticism toward biometric interventions expressed by participants invites further reflection on the assumptions underlying Smart Health research, pointing to the possibility that technological nudges may need to be accompanied by structural and psychological support to produce meaningful behavioral change.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
